   (ISEST), Dalian, PEOPLES R CHINA, JUN 04-07, 2013},
Organization = {Chinese Soc Environm Sci; Beijing Inst Technol; Dalian Univ Technol;
   Dalian Assoc Sci \& Technol; ENVR Div Amer Chem Soc; Dalian Soc Environm
   Sci, Profess Comm Pollut Prevent \& Control Heavy Metals CSES; Dalian
   Univ Technol, Key Lab Ind Ecol \& Environm Engn; Dalian Univ Technol,
   Sch Environm Sci \& Technol; Beijing Inst Technol, State Key Lab Explos
   Sci \& Technol},
Abstract = {Stormwater draining from impervious surfaces in urban areas often
   contains readily soluble salts in runoff, which contribute dramatically
   to the degradation of aquatic ecosystem. The concentrations of potassium
   (K), cadmium (Ca), sodium (Na), magnesium (Mg), chloride (Cl) and
   sulfate (SO42-) in stormwater runoff in the urban areas of Shenyang city
   were measured in 4 rainfall events during July 2009 to June 2010. The
   results showed that there was an obvious increasing of salt ions
   concentrations in stormwater runoff samples, especially Na and Cl. It is
   also found that readily soluble salts ions in stormwater runoff peaked
   at the first storm event after winter. This study demonstrated that the
   applied deicing salts in winter significantly contributed to the
   increasing concentrations of K, Ca, Na, Mg, Cl, and SO42- in urban
   stormwater runoff. It is evident that the application of deicing salts
   in cold regions of China every year has had adverse impacts on the
   aquatic ecology through stormwater runoff. (C) 2013 The Authors.
   Published by Elsevier B.V.},
