Note = {International Conference on Business, Management, Tourism and
   Hospitality (BIZMATOUR), Malacca, MALAYSIA, MAY 12-14, 2015},
Abstract = {The long awaited IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers has
   finally been jointly released by FASB and IASB in May 2013. This
   standard becomes effective from 1 January 2017 and will replace the
   present revenue standards that are in practice. The five-step revenue
   recognition model has shifted from the concept of ``risks and
   rewards{''} currently in practice to the ``concept of control.{''} This
   is expected to have impact on industries, specifically, those involving
   long-term contracts with customers since the timing and manner of
   revenue recognition may be affected. As revenue is an important element
   in the financial statements, it is important that the implementation of
   this new standard is free from ambiguity and is robust to be applied
   across different sectors. This paper, hence, provides an overview of
   Malaysian preparers and auditors' perception on IFRS 15. Results of the
   study revealed that generally Malaysian accountants surveyed are still
   not ready to adopt IFRS 15 and they perceived that the standard is not
   easy to be applied across different business sectors.},
