Note = {5th International Conference on Energy, Environment, Ecosystems and
   Sustainable Development/2nd International Conference on Landscape
   Architecture, Vouliagmeni, GREECE, SEP 28-30, 2009},
Abstract = {Low Impact Development (LID) is a way to mitigate the negative effects
   of increasing urbanization and impervious surfaces. The aquatic buffer
   is known as one of the LID approaches, and the significance benefits of
   them aresurface runoff decrease, water quality and ecosystem protection,
   and enhancement of groundwater recharge. Although the satisfactory
   performance of the artificial lake to enhance groundwater level has been
   reported by previous studies, there is no standard design guideline
   available for aquatic buffers for groundwater recharge. In this study we
   suggest principal design factor for the aquatic buffers to enhance
   groundwater recharge, especially for pond/lake buffers, and present the
   scheme of developing construction design and maintenance guidelines for
   artificial lake in urban planning. A design guideline for artificial
   lake can be a new approach for urban water environment management,
   helping manage urban water environment, and introduce an ecological
   engineering viewpoint throughout the construction industry to achieve
   sustainable development.},
