   Microorganisms into the Environment - Contamination Pathways, Health
   Effects and Control, Univ Salerno, Fisciano, ITALY, JUN 24, 2011},
Organization = {Italian Grp Environm \& Sanitary Engn (GITISA)},
Abstract = {A municipal wastewater secondary effluent from a biological treatment
   was ozonated in order to study the efficiency in the organic
   micropollutants removal and in the simultaneous changes of the effluents
   organic matter characteristics. Two ozone doses based in the amount of
   dissolved organic carbon (DOC), around 2.6 and 13 mg O-3/mgDOC, were
   applied, and the effluent was analyzed before and after ozonation. Ozone
   confirmed its efficiency in terms of micropollutant reduction when
   applied to the municipal biotreated effluent. Furthermore, organic
   matter transformation was also investigating through the evolution of
   lumped parameters as chemical oxygen demand (COD), dissolved organic
   matter (DOC), UV254 absorbance and the fate dissolved organic fractions
   by means of size exclusion chromatography followed by organic carbon
   detection. According to the results obtained, it was observed that the
   oxidation of micropollutants competes with the oxidation of the bulk of
   organic matter and this organic matter was converted into more
   biodegradable, less aromatic and smaller substances.},
