Note = {2nd International Conference on Advanced Science and Information
   Technology (ASCIT), Cheonan, SOUTH KOREA, MAY 12-13, 2017},
Abstract = {As identity marker, food is related to certain area which is
   specifically different. Javanese coastal culinary tradition has peculiar
   methods and groceries that have been inherited through generations. On
   the other hand, the American fast food restaurants currently have
   entered Indonesian in the districts and small town areas. To some
   extent, their existence is regarded as a threat over the local coastal
   traditional food. This paper aims to analyze the perception of
   Indonesian young generation on Javanese coastal culinary tradition and
   American fast food. By comparing the youths' perception and acceptance
   on both foods, this study used primary data by distributing
   questionnaires and conducting Focus Group Discussion (FGD) among 50
   (fifty) students of Diponegoro University, Indonesia who come from
   coastal areas in Central Java. The result shows that the respondents
   have positive perception both on Javanese coastal culinary tradition and
   American fast food. The majority do not feel outdated when consuming
   coastal traditional food, however, they feel more modern when having
   American fast food. They also show that their acceptance also applies on
   Javanese coastal culinary tradition, but there is a trend showing that
   their acceptance on American fast food is lower. It may be endorsed by
   the political indoctrination. Therefore, from the pragmatism view
   respondents show their interests in both types of foods, whereas from
   identity politics they seem to keep some distances from American fast
   food.},
